schema: "0.4"
id: "fim-audio-and-sound-tone_js-02-procedural-sound-design"
type: audio
quality: high
solution: tone_js
category: audio-and-sound
variant: procedural-sound-design
references:
  - "references/fim/overview/music-audio-ml/tone_js.md"
  - "references/fim/solution/tone_js.md"
prompt:
  system: |-
    Generate production-quality Tone.js browser audio assets for FIM evaluation. Output only the requested source artifact, with no markdown fences or explanatory prose.
  text: |-
    Create a self-contained Tone.js procedural sound design artifact for the audio-and-sound category.

    Scenario:
    Build a compact browser sound lab for game and product effects. It must synthesize at least six labeled cues, such as confirm, deny, pickup, impact, power-up, alarm, ambience, and transition sweep, without relying on external audio files. Each cue should demonstrate procedural synthesis, envelopes, modulation, filtering, panning, and reusable routing.

    Implementation requirements:
    - Use Tone.js 15 from a CDN or an equivalent import pattern.
    - Unlock audio with a user gesture through await Tone.start(); no sound may play on page load.
    - Use Tone.MembraneSynth, Tone.NoiseSynth, Tone.MetalSynth, Tone.FMSynth, Tone.AMSynth, MonoSynth, Oscillator, or Player only when the source is generated or has a visible fallback.
    - Use reusable synth and effect objects instead of creating a new permanent node for every trigger.
    - Use Tone.Envelope, Tone.Filter, Tone.LFO, Tone.Panner, Tone.Gain, Tone.Channel, or Tone.Compressor to shape sound design parameters.
    - Expose safe controls for intensity, pitch, decay, wet mix, and master volume with bounded ranges and accessible labels.
    - Schedule any layered cue components with Tone.now(), the trigger time argument, or Tone.Transport; do not use timer jitter for cue timing.
    - Include gain staging for stacked effects, with peak-safe defaults and a limiter or compressor before Tone.Destination.
    - Include cleanup and dispose behavior for all instruments, modulators, effects, scheduled events, and UI listeners.
    - Include fallback checks for unavailable Web Audio, failed Tone.js load, muted output, suspended context, and reduced sound preference.
    - Output only a complete HTML document with inline CSS and JavaScript.
  requirements:
    - "Autoplay policy: all cue preview buttons are silent until Tone.start() succeeds from a user gesture."
    - "Gain staging: every cue routes through controlled gains and a final dynamics stage before destination."
    - "Timing: layered cue events use Tone scheduling primitives or Tone.now() offsets, not unsynchronized timers."
    - "Cleanup/dispose: destroy releases synths, effects, LFOs, panners, gains, and event handlers."
    - "Fallback checks: unsupported or suspended audio shows actionable status and leaves the UI stable."
  provider_options:
    max_tokens: 8192
    temperature: 0.2
output:
  text_format: html
  artifact: "Tone.js procedural sound design browser audio source"
  formats:
    - format: html
    - format: js
  render_expectation: "Interactive browser sound palette with procedural cue playback after audio unlock and visible diagnostics."
evaluation:
  checklist:
    - "YAML parses under schema 0.4, all required fields are present, and all file text is ASCII-only."
    - "Generated artifact is a complete Tone.js HTML or JavaScript browser asset with no markdown fences."
    - "At least six named procedural cues are generated from Tone.js synths, noise, envelopes, filters, modulation, panning, or effects."
    - "Autoplay policy is respected with await Tone.start() inside a user gesture before any cue plays."
    - "Gain staging prevents clipping when cues overlap and includes bounded master and cue levels."
    - "Timing for layered cue components uses Tone scheduling, Tone.now() offsets, or Transport time."
    - "Controls are bounded, accessible, keyboard usable, and do not allow unsafe gain or runaway feedback."
    - "Cleanup/dispose frees synths, effects, modulation nodes, scheduled events, and UI listeners."
    - "Fallback checks cover unsupported Web Audio, failed Tone.js load, muted output, suspended context, and reduced sound preference."
  rubric:
    - name: "Format validity"
      weight: 30
      criteria: "YAML parses, schema 0.4 fields are present, generated output is valid HTML or JavaScript, and file name, id, solution, category, type, and variant align."
    - name: "Semantic completeness"
      weight: 30
      criteria: "The artifact fulfills the procedural sound design scenario and uses Tone.js synthesis, envelopes, modulation, effects, autoplay unlock, and disposal patterns from the references."
    - name: "Audio/structural quality"
      weight: 25
      criteria: "The cues are distinct, intentional, well labeled, dynamically controlled, and free of obvious clicks, clipping, or timing smear."
    - name: "Robustness"
      weight: 15
      criteria: "The artifact handles browser autoplay, overlapping cues, unsupported audio, repeated playback, cleanup, responsive UI, and accessibility states without runtime failure."
  pass_conditions:
    - "Rubric weights sum to 100 with Format validity 30, Semantic completeness 30, Audio/structural quality 25, and Robustness 15."
    - "Score is at least 80 out of 100."
    - "No critical failure in YAML parsing, Tone.js loading, autoplay unlock, gain staging, timing, cleanup/dispose, or fallback checks."
    - "Generated content is clearly aligned with Tone.js procedural sound design and does not depend on hidden external media."
tags:
  - "fim"
  - "audio-and-sound"
  - "tone_js"
  - "procedural-sound-design"
  - "sound-effects"
  - "synthesis"
  - "browser-audio"
